latest_plan = 'Based on our reflection, here\'s a step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Initialize the output grid:\n   - Create a 4x7 grid filled with zeros (black cells).\n\n2. Process the input grid in 4x2 sections:\n   - Iterate through the input grid, considering each 4x2 section (7 sections total).\n\n3. For each 4x2 section:\n   a. Count the total number of colored cells (2 or 3) in the section.\n   \n   b. Check for vertical lines:\n      - Look for vertical lines of 3 or 4 colored cells.\n      - If found, mark those specific rows in the corresponding output column as gray (5).\n   \n   c. Check for horizontal pairs:\n      - Look for rows with exactly 2 colored cells.\n      - If found, mark that specific row in the corresponding output column as gray (5).\n   \n   d. Check total colored cells:\n      - If the total count of colored cells is 3 or more, mark the entire corresponding output column as gray (5).\n\n4. Post-processing:\n   - Iterate through the output grid.\n   - If a cell has gray cells (5) on both its left and right, change it to gray (5) as well.\n\n5. Return the processed output grid.\n\nHere\'s how we would implement this plan:\n\n```python\nfrom rob_agi.colored_grid import ColoredGrid\n\ndef solve_66f2d22f(input_grid: ColoredGrid) -> ColoredGrid:\n    output_grid = ColoredGrid(values=[[0 for _ in range(7)] for _ in range(4)])\n    \n    def process_section(section: List[List[int]]) -> List[int]:\n        result = [0, 0, 0, 0]\n        colored_count = sum(cell in [2, 3] for row in section for cell in row)\n        \n        # Check for vertical lines\n        for col in range(2):\n            vertical_line = [row[col] in [2, 3] for row in section]\n            if sum(vertical_line) >= 3:\n                for i, is_colored in enumerate(vertical_line):\n                    if is_colored:\n                        result[i] = 5\n        \n        # Check for horizontal pairs\n        for row in range(4):\n            if sum(cell in [2, 3] for cell in section[row]) == 2:\n                result[row] = 5\n        \n        # Check total colored cells\n        if colored_count >= 3:\n            return [5, 5, 5, 5]\n        \n        return result\n\n    # Process each section\n    for i in range(7):\n        section = [row[2*i:2*i+2] for row in input_grid.values]\n        output_column = process_section(section)\n        for row in range(4):\n            output_grid.values[row][i] = output_column[row]\n    \n    # Post-processing\n    for row in range(4):\n        for col in range(1, 6):\n            if output_grid.values[row][col-1] == 5 and output_grid.values[row][col+1] == 5:\n                output_grid.values[row][col] = 5\n    \n    return output_grid\n```\n\nThis implementation follows our step-by-step plan and should correctly solve the challenge for all given test cases. It captures the essence of identifying "significant color presence" in various configurations within each 4x2 section of the input grid.'
